{
  "$schema": "./.claude/scripts/framework/manifest-schema.json",
  "name": "IDPF Framework",
  "version": "0.105.0",
  "description": "Interactive Development Process Frameworks for AI-assisted software development",
  "consumerCompatibility": {
    "praxisHubManager": {
      "manifestContract": 2,
      "contractIntroduced": "0.104.0",
      "reason": "Contract 2 replaced extensibleCommands, managedCommands and deploymentFiles.commands.{core,workflow,managed} with the single deploymentFiles.commands.registry (#2942). A hub that reads only the retired keys finds none of them.",
      "detectableBy": "Two directions, and BOTH must be implemented. UPWARD (manifest ahead of consumer): compare this manifestContract against the highest contract the consumer implements and refuse, or warn, when the manifest is ahead. DOWNWARD (consumer ahead of manifest): a manifest with no consumerCompatibility key is contract 1 — see absentContractMeans — and the consumer serves it on the contract-1 read path (top-level extensibleCommands / managedCommands and deploymentFiles.commands.core/.workflow) instead of refusing it.",
      "limitation": "Scoped to the UPWARD direction and NOT retroactive, which is the field's central caveat rather than a gap in it. A consumer predating contract 2 does not read this field either, so it cannot self-detect: it reads the retired keys, finds them absent, and — if it coalesces with `|| []` — deploys zero commands while reporting success. That silent failure is what the field makes impossible from contract 2 onward and cannot prevent for versions already shipped. It is also the one real ordering constraint left: a contract-2 consumer must ship NO LATER than the first manifest declaring contract 2, because old-consumer-meets-new-manifest is the only combination neither direction can detect. The reverse order is safe — backwardCompatibility makes a new consumer serve old manifests.",
      "absentContractMeans": 1,
      "backwardCompatibility": "A consumer implementing contract N MUST keep serving every contract below N. This is PERMANENT, not transitional: installed hub roots are frozen per version (px-manager#831) and cannot be repointed retroactively, so contract-1 hubs never age out and a coordinated release cannot retire them. Refusing a contract-1 manifest is a DEFECT, not a safety measure — Praxis Hub Manager serves several hub versions at once, so a consumer that refuses contract 1 refuses every hub installed before 0.104.0, which is every hub that exists today. Fail loudly on a contract-2 manifest whose registry is missing — that is corruption. Never on a manifest that is merely older."
    }
  },
  "created": "2025-11-30",
  "frameworks": [
    "IDPF-Agile"
  ],
  "runtimeNpmDependencies": {
    "ajv": "^8.18.0",
    "ajv-formats": "^3.0.1",
    "serve": "^14.2.6",
    "yaml": "^2.8.3"
  },
